(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test02_1A"] = [{"id":"849bf8d7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In the mid-nineteenth century, some abolitionist newspapers ______ westward migration in the United States; by printing a letter that described the easy fortunes and high salaries miners could make in California during the Gold Rush, Frederick Douglass’s newspaper North Star was one such publication that inspired readers to relocate.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["stimulated","assigned","opposed","disregarded"]},{"id":"aa7fc89b","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is adapted from Susan Glaspell’s 1912 short story “‘Out There.’” An elderly shop owner is looking at a picture that he recently acquired and hopes to sell. It did seem that the picture failed to fit in with the rest of the shop. A persuasive young fellow who claimed he was closing out his stock let the old man have it for what he called a song. It was only a little out-of-the-way store which subsisted chiefly on the framing of pictures. The old man looked around at his views of the city, his pictures of cats and dogs, his flaming bits of landscape. “Don’t belong in here,” he fumed. And yet the old man was secretly proud of his acquisition. There was a hidden dignity in his scowling as he shuffled about pondering the least ridiculous place for the picture.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Susan Glaspell’s 1912 short story “‘Out There.’” An elderly shop owner is looking at a picture that he recently","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"acquired and hopes to sell.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"It did seem that the picture failed to fit in with the rest of the shop. A persuasive young fellow who claimed he was closing out his stock let the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"old man have it for what he called a song. It was only a little out-of-the-way store which subsisted chiefly on the framing of pictures. The old","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"man looked around at his views of the city, his pictures of cats and dogs, his flaming bits of landscape. “Don’t belong in here,” he fumed.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And yet the old man was secretly proud of his acquisition. There was a hidden dignity in his scowling as he shuffled about pondering the least","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"ridiculous place for the picture.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To reveal the shop owner’s conflicted feelings about the new picture","To convey the shop owner’s resentment of the person he got the new picture from","To describe the items that the shop owner most highly prizes","To explain differences between the new picture and other pictures in the shop"]},{"id":"95f037ad","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The works of Chicana artist Ester Hernandez are now ______ in museums both in the United States and abroad, but the murals she contributed to as a member of Las Mujeres Muralistas early in her artistic career were displayed in outdoor public spaces across San Francisco.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The works of Chicana artist Ester Hernandez are now ______ in museums both in the United States and abroad, but the murals she contributed to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"as a member of Las Mujeres Muralistas early in her artistic career were displayed in outdoor public spaces across San Francisco.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["invented","adjusted","featured","recommended"]},{"id":"b940952c","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"On the basis of extensive calculations and models, astronomers in the 1990s predicted that the collision of two neutron stars or a neutron star and a black hole could release a massive burst of gamma rays in an event called a kilonova. This ______ was confirmed with observations in 2017.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"On the basis of extensive calculations and models, astronomers in the 1990s predicted that the collision of two neutron stars or a neutron star","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and a black hole could release a massive burst of gamma rays in an event called a kilonova. This ______ was confirmed with observations in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"2017.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["theory","evidence","constant","experiment"]},{"id":"62a18353","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Zora Neale Hurston’s 1921 short story “John Redding Goes to Sea.” John wants to travel far beyond the village where he lives near his mother, Matty. [John] had on several occasions attempted to reconcile his mother to the notion, but found it a difficult task. Matty always took refuge in self-pity and tears. Her son’s desires were incomprehensible to her, that was all. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Zora Neale Hurston’s 1921 short story “John Redding Goes to Sea.” John wants to travel far beyond the village","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"where he lives near his mother, Matty.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"[John] had on several occasions attempted to reconcile his mother to the notion, but found it a difficult task. Matty always took","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"refuge in self-pity and tears. Her son’s desires were incomprehensible to her, that was all.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the phrase “reconcile his mother to” most nearly mean?","choices":["Get his mother to accept","Get his mother to apologize for","Get his mother to match","Get his mother to reunite with"],"underline":"reconcile his mother to","underline_segments":["reconcile his mother to"]},{"id":"93665100","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Seminole/Muscogee director Sterlin Harjo ______ television’s tendency to situate Native characters in the distant past: this rejection is evident in his series Reservation Dogs, which revolves around teenagers who dress in contemporary styles and whose dialogue is laced with current slang.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Seminole/Muscogee director Sterlin Harjo ______ television’s tendency to situate Native characters in the distant past: this rejection is evident in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"his series Reservation Dogs, which revolves around teenagers who dress in contemporary styles and whose dialogue is laced with current slang.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["repudiates","proclaims","foretells","recants"]},{"id":"edd64a4a","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Charles Chesnutt’s 1899 story “The Wife of His Youth.” Mr. Ryder is hosting a formal gathering where he will propose marriage to the woman he has been courting. [A] younger and less cautious man would long since have spoken. But he had made up his mind, and had only to determine the time when he would ask her to be his wife. He decided to give a ball in her honor, and at some time during the evening of the ball to offer her his heart and hand. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Charles Chesnutt’s 1899 story “The Wife of His Youth.” Mr. Ryder is hosting a formal gathering where he will","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"propose marriage to the woman he has been courting.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"[A] younger and less cautious man would long since have spoken. But he had made up his mind, and had only to determine the","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"time when he would ask her to be his wife. He decided to give a ball in her honor, and at some time during the evening of the ball","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"to offer her his heart and hand.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “determine” most nearly mean?","choices":["Choose","Influence","Demonstrate","Measure"],"underline":"determine","underline_segments":["determine"]},{"id":"adc8ea28","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The fossil remains of the individual known as Oase 1, discovered in Romania in 2002, can help paleoanthropologists not only ______ steps in the evolution of hominids but also illuminate the Pleistocene epoch generally, revealing important details about the time in which Oase 1 lived.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The fossil remains of the individual known as Oase 1, discovered in Romania in 2002, can help paleoanthropologists not only ______ steps in the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"evolution of hominids but also illuminate the Pleistocene epoch generally, revealing important details about the time in which Oase 1 lived.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["exploit","discern","prioritize","yield"]},{"id":"daa90829","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Fanny Burney’s 1778 novel Evelina. The text is an excerpt from a letter by Lady Howard to Evelina’s guardian, Reverend Villars, in which Lady Howard discusses an upcoming trip to London planned by her daughter Mrs. Mirvan’s family. It is very earnestly [the Mirvans ] wish to enlarge and enliven their party by the addition of your amiable ward, who would share, equally with her own daughter, the care and attention of Mrs. Mirvan. Do not start at this proposal; it is time that [Evelina] should see something of the world. When young people are too rigidly sequestered from it, their lively and romantic imaginations paint it to them as a paradise of which they have been beguiled; but when they are shown it properly, and in due time, they see it such as it really is, equally shared by pain and pleasure, hope and disappointment.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Fanny Burney’s 1778 novel Evelina. The text is an excerpt from a letter by Lady Howard to Evelina’s guardian,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Reverend Villars, in which Lady Howard discusses an upcoming trip to London planned by her daughter Mrs. Mirvan’s family.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"It is very earnestly [the Mirvans’] wish to enlarge and enliven their party by the addition of your amiable ward, who would share,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"equally with her own daughter, the care and attention of Mrs. Mirvan. Do not start at this proposal; it is time that [Evelina] should","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"see something of the world. When young people are too rigidly sequestered from it, their lively and romantic imaginations paint it","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"to them as a paradise of which they have been beguiled; but when they are shown it properly, and in due time, they see it such as it","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"really is, equally shared by pain and pleasure, hope and disappointment.","indent":2}],"question":"Based on the text, Lady Howard would most likely agree with which statement about Reverend Villars?","choices":["Although the manner in which he has raised Evelina is in many ways exemplary, he has been misguided in shielding her from the influence of other young people.","He has imparted to Evelina his own idealistic view of the world, which results in her being unprepared to face inevitable disappointments.","Although his desire to guard Evelina from unscrupulous people is commendable, his general mistrust has led him to be unduly wary of the Mirvans.","He is overly protective of Evelina, who would likely benefit from a greater variety of experiences than she has had thus far."]},{"id":"5c7e0d62","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Arthurian legends (tales related to the character of King Arthur) derive from many sources, such as Vita Sancti Cadoci, composed in the 11th century, and Culhwch and Olwen from the second half of the 12th century. One of the most significant sources, Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History of the Kings of Britain, was written in the 1130s; some material from it was later adapted by the Norman poet Wace into the Roman de Brut in 1155. But Wace didn’t merely adapt History, he added to it as well, introducing the famous Round Table at which Arthur’s knights assembled, which suggests that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Arthurian legends (tales related to the character of King Arthur) derive from many sources, such as Vita Sancti Cadoci, composed in the 11th","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"century, and Culhwch and Olwen from the second half of the 12th century. One of the most significant sources, Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the Kings of Britain, was written in the 1130s; some material from it was later adapted by the Norman poet Wace into the Roman de Brut in 1155.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"But Wace didn’t merely adapt History, he added to it as well, introducing the famous Round Table at which Arthur’s knights assembled, which","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"suggests that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["Roman de Brut is more historically accurate than History, because Culhwch and Olwen had not been written when Geoffrey of Monmouth was writing his work.","Geoffrey of Monmouth was unaware of stories of the Round Table when composing his History, though historians know that works containing such stories were available to him.","Geoffrey of Monmouth’s accounts of Arthurian legends in his History are more similar overall in content to the accounts in Culhwch and Olwen than they are to the accounts in Roman de Brut.","the Arthurian legends that the author of Vita Sancti Cadoci drew on would not have featured the Round Table."]},{"id":"22b3da87","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"During the Bourbon Restoration in France (1814 –1830), the right to vote required in part that a person paid at least 300 francs in direct taxes to the government. The four most common taxes (the quatre vieilles) were levied on real estate (both land and buildings); the doors and windows in taxpayer homes; the rental values of homes; and the businesses of artisans and merchants. (Foreign investments were either exempt from taxation or taxed lightly.) Although relatively few people paid the tax on real estate, it was the main means of voter qualification and accounted for over two-thirds of government receipts during this period, suggesting that during the Bourbon Restoration ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"During the Bourbon Restoration in France (1814–1830), the right to vote required in part that a person paid at least 300 francs in direct taxes to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the government. The four most common taxes (the quatre vieilles) were levied on real estate (both land and buildings); the doors and windows in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"taxpayer homes; the rental values of homes; and the businesses of artisans and merchants. (Foreign investments were either exempt from","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"taxation or taxed lightly.) Although relatively few people paid the tax on real estate, it was the main means of voter qualification and accounted","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"for over two-thirds of government receipts during this period, suggesting that during the Bourbon Restoration ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["those people who had the right to vote most likely had substantial holdings of French real estate.","the voting habits of French artisans and merchants were effective in reducing tax burdens on businesses.","the number of doors and windows in French residences was kept to a minimum but increased after 1830.","French people with significant foreign investments were unlikely to have the right to vote."]},{"id":"22e4d633","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Although many transposons, DNA sequences that move within an organism’s genome through shuffling or duplication, have become corrupted and inactive over time, those from the long interspersed nuclear elements (LINE) family appear to remain active in the genomes of some species. In humans, they are functionally important within the hippocampus, a brain structure that supports complex cognitive processes. When the results of molecular analysis of two species of octopus— an animal known for its intelligence — were announced in 2022, the confirmation of a LINE transposon in Octopus vulgaris and Octopus bimaculoides genomes prompted researchers to hypothesize that that transposon family is tied to a species capacity for advanced cognition. Which finding, if true, would most directly support","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Although many transposons, DNA sequences that move within an organism’s genome through shuffling or duplication, have become corrupted","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and inactive over time, those from the long interspersed nuclear elements (LINE) family appear to remain active in the genomes of some","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"species. In humans, they are functionally important within the hippocampus, a brain structure that supports complex cognitive processes. When","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the results of molecular analysis of two species of octopus—an animal known for its intelligence—were announced in 2022, the confirmation of a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"LINE transposon in Octopus vulgaris and Octopus bimaculoides genomes prompted researchers to hypothesize that that transposon family is tied","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"to a species’ capacity for advanced cognition.","indent":0}],"question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers’ hypothesis?","choices":["The LINE transposon in O. vulgaris and O. bimaculoides genomes is active in an octopus brain structure that functions similarly to the human hippocampus.","The human genome contains multiple transposons from the LINE family that are all primarily active in the hippocampus.","A consistent number of copies of LINE transposons is present across the genomes of most octopus species, with few known corruptions.","O. vulgaris and O. bimaculoides have smaller brains than humans do relative to body size, but their genomes contain sequences from a wider variety of transposon families."]},{"id":"bc7b1a04","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Three Studies' Estimated Average Velocity of LMC Researchers Study year Estimated average velocity Murai and Fujimoto 1980 344 km/s Kallivayalil and colleagues 2006 378 km/s Gardiner and colleagues 1994 297 km/s In 2006, Nitya Kallivayalil and colleagues calculated the most accurate estimate yet of the average velocity (in kilometers per second) of the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) galaxy. Before the 2006 study, estimates of the average velocity were low enough for the LMC to maintain an orbit around the Milky Way galaxy, but according to an analysis by Gurtina Besla and colleagues, the estimated velocity from the 2006 study is too high for the LMC to maintain such an orbit. Therefore, if Besla and colleagues are correct, the maximum average velocity for the LMC that would allow it to maintain orbit around the Milky Way is likely ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Three Studies’ Estimated Average Velocity of LMC","headers":["Researchers","Study year","Estimated average velocity"],"rows":[["Murai and Fujimoto","1980","344 km/s"],["Kallivayalil and colleagues","2006","378 km/s"],["Gardiner and colleagues","1994","297 km/s"]]},{"type":"text","text":"In 2006, Nitya Kallivayalil and colleagues calculated the most accurate estimate yet of the average velocity (in kilometers per second) of the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) galaxy. Before the 2006 study, estimates of the average velocity were low enough for the LMC to maintain an orbit around the Milky Way galaxy, but according to an analysis by Gurtina Besla and colleagues, the estimated velocity from the 2006 study is too high for the LMC to maintain such an orbit. Therefore, if Besla and colleagues are correct, the maximum average velocity for the LMC that would allow it to maintain orbit around the Milky Way is likely ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["above 344 km/s but below 378 km/s.","above 297 km/s but below 344 km/s.","above 378 km/s.","below 297 km/s."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Three Studies'","Estimated Average","Velocity of LMC"]},{"id":"e47d2524","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic waste. Areas of the ocean with higher concentrations of microplastic particles also have smaller and fewer waves. A study by Yukun Sun and colleagues found that the concentration of microplastic particles cannot be the only reason for this reduced wave activity because the concentration of particles that would have the observed effect is much higher than that found in these areas of the ocean. However, they found that surfactants, chemicals often used to manufacture plastics, are released into the water from microplastics and have a much stronger wave-reducing effect.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic waste. Areas of the ocean with higher concentrations of microplastic particles also have smaller and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"fewer waves. A study by Yukun Sun and colleagues found that the concentration of microplastic particles cannot be the only reason for this","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"reduced wave activity because the concentration of particles that would have the observed effect is much higher than that found in these areas","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of the ocean. However, they found that surfactants, chemicals often used to manufacture plastics, are released into the water from microplastics","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and have a much stronger wave-reducing effect.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, what did Sun and colleagues discover about surfactants?","choices":["They have a much stronger effect on wave activity than microplastics alone do.","They are mainly composed of water.","They are helpful for removing microplastics from the ocean.","They can be used to contain microplastics within certain areas of the ocean."]},{"id":"95dbdf51","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Laura Mulvey has theorized that in narrative film, shots issuing from a protagonist’s point of view compel viewers to identify with the character. Such identification is heightened by “invisible editing,” or editing so inconspicuous that it renders cuts between shots almost unnoticeable. Conversely, Mulvey proposes that conspicuous editing or an absence of point-of-view shots would induce a more critical stance toward a protagonist. Consider, for example, the attic scene in Alfred Hitchcock’s The Birds, a conspicuously edited sequence of tens of shots, few of which correspond to the protagonist’s point of view. According to Mulvey’s logic, this scene should affect viewers by ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Laura Mulvey has theorized that in narrative film, shots issuing from a protagonist’s point of view compel viewers to identify with the character.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Such identification is heightened by “invisible editing,” or editing so inconspicuous that it renders cuts between shots almost unnoticeable.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Conversely, Mulvey proposes that conspicuous editing or an absence of point-of-view shots would induce a more critical stance toward a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"protagonist. Consider, for example, the attic scene in Alfred Hitchcock’s The Birds, a conspicuously edited sequence of tens of shots, few of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"which correspond to the protagonist’s point of view. According to Mulvey’s logic, this scene should affect viewers by ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["obscuring their awareness of the high degree of artifice involved in constructing the montage.","lessening their identification with the protagonist, if not alienating them from the character altogether.","compelling them to identify with the film’s director, whose proxy is the camera, and not with the protagonist.","diverting their attention away from the film’s content and toward its stylistic attributes."]},{"id":"2c49940e","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"French philosopher Ren é Descartes doubted whether he could prove his own existence. Eventually, he found proof in his famous phrase I think, “therefore I am.” The ______ complexity: only those who exist would be able to ponder their existence.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"French philosopher René Descartes doubted whether he could prove his own existence. Eventually, he found proof in his famous phrase “I think,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"therefore I am.” The ______ complexity: only those who exist would be able to ponder their existence.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["phrases’ simplicity masks its","phrases simplicity masks their","phrase’s simplicity masks their","phrase’s simplicity masks its"]},{"id":"77bf77cd","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Farouk El-Baz, a geologist and space scientist, ______ part of the team that selected the lunar landing sites for the Apollo program during the 1960s and 1970s.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Farouk El-Baz, a geologist and space scientist, ______ part of the team that selected the lunar landing sites for the Apollo program during the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"1960s and 1970s.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are","was","have been","were"]},{"id":"b260c65a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Earth is not a perfect sphere. Due to the ______ gravitational pull, Earth bulges out on the sides closest to and farthest from the Moon. This distorting pull is known as a tidal force, and it is responsible for the changes in water levels that are called high and low tides.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Earth is not a perfect sphere. Due to the ______ gravitational pull, Earth bulges out on the sides closest to and farthest from the Moon. This","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"distorting pull is known as a tidal force, and it is responsible for the changes in water levels that are called high and low tides.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Moon’s","Moons","Moons’","Moon"]},{"id":"1f8cd95f","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"In the 1950s, a man named Joseph McVicker was struggling to keep his business afloat when his sister-in-law Kay Zufall advised him to repurpose the company’s product, a nontoxic, clay-like substance for removing soot from wallpaper, as a modeling putty for kids. In addition, Zufall ______ selling the product under a child-friendly name: Play-Doh.","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["suggested","suggests","had suggested","was suggesting"]},{"id":"eef91a50","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Nine months before Rosa Parks made history by refusing to comply with the segregated seating policy on a Montgomery, Alabama, bus, a fifteen-year-old Montgomery girl named Claudette Colvin was arrested for the same ______ to some historians, Colvin’s arrest led to Parks’s action and eventually to the desegregation of Montgomery’s bus system.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Nine months before Rosa Parks made history by refusing to comply with the segregated seating policy on a Montgomery, Alabama, bus, a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"fifteen-year-old Montgomery girl named Claudette Colvin was arrested for the same ______ to some historians, Colvin’s arrest led to Parks’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"action and eventually to the desegregation of Montgomery’s bus system.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["offense. According","offense, according","offense according","offense and according"]},{"id":"d4fe8f03","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Paintings by the renowned twentieth-century US ______ were featured in Artist to Artist, an exhibition at the Smithsonian Art Museum that paired the works of artists whose career trajectories intersected in meaningful ways.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Paintings by the renowned twentieth-century US ______ were featured in Artist to Artist, an exhibition at the Smithsonian Art Museum that paired","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the works of artists whose career trajectories intersected in meaningful ways.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["artists: Thomas Hart Benton and Jackson Pollock,","artists Thomas Hart Benton and Jackson Pollock","artists Thomas Hart Benton, and Jackson Pollock,","artists, Thomas Hart Benton and Jackson Pollock"]},{"id":"60917233","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In the 1880s, inventor Lewis Latimer improved upon Thomas Edison’s design for the electric light bulb. ______ Latimer made the light bulb more durable by placing cardboard around its carbon filament. With this innovation, Latimer became the first Black inventor to contribute to the electrification of the world.","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Soon,","Regardless,","However,","Specifically,"]},{"id":"5b8b69a2","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Archaeologist Jon Erlandson and colleagues argue that humans first arrived in the Americas by sea. They propose that humans traveled between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines from northeast Asia to the Americas. Many of these islands and coastal zones were later submerged as glaciers melted and sea levels rose. The researchers think that “a coastal route, including kelp forests and estuaries, would have provided a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources” such as seaweeds, fish, and birds. This proposed scenario is known as the kelp highway hypothesis. The student wants to summarize the kelp highway hypothesis.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Archaeologist Jon Erlandson and colleagues argue that humans first arrived in the Americas by sea.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"They propose that humans traveled between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines from northeast Asia to the Americas.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Many of these islands and coastal zones were later submerged as glaciers melted and sea levels rose.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The researchers think that “a coastal route, including kelp forests and estuaries, would have provided a rich mix of marine, estuarine,","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"riverine, and terrestrial resources” such as seaweeds, fish, and birds.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This proposed scenario is known as the kelp highway hypothesis.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines likely contained “a rich mix of marine, estuarine, riverine, and terrestrial resources” such as seaweeds, fish, and birds, according to researchers.","One argument about how humans first arrived in the Americas is the kelp highway hypothesis proposed by Jon Erlandson and colleagues.","Humans may have first arrived in the Americas by sea, traveling between Pacific Ocean islands and coastlines and subsisting on a variety of resources.","As glaciers melted and sea levels rose, many Pacific Ocean islands and coastal zones were submerged."]},{"id":"81315093","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: A marathon is a long-distance running race that is 26.2 miles long. An ultramarathon is a long-distance running race of more than 26.2 miles. The Kepler Challenge is a one-day, 37.3-mile ultramarathon in New Zealand. The Spreelauf is a six-day, 261-mile ultramarathon in Germany. The student wants to make a generalization about ultramarathons.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"A marathon is a long-distance running race that is 26.2 miles long.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"An ultramarathon is a long-distance running race of more than 26.2 miles.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The Kepler Challenge is a one-day, 37.3-mile ultramarathon in New Zealand.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"The Spreelauf is a six-day, 261-mile ultramarathon in Germany.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Examples of ultramarathons include the 37.3-mile Kepler Challenge in New Zealand and the 261-mile Spreelauf in Germany.","A marathon is 26.2 miles long, but the Spreelauf ultramarathon, at 261 miles, is far longer.","Ultramarathons range widely in length, from a few dozen miles to a few hundred.","While the Kepler Challenge is a one-day ultramarathon, the Spreelauf is a six-day ultramarathon."]},{"id":"9e34720b","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Although those who migrated to California in 1849 dreamed of finding gold nuggets in streambeds, the state’s richest deposits were buried deeply in rock, beyond the reach of individual prospectors. ______ by 1852, many had given up their fortune-hunting dreams and gone to work for one of the large companies capable of managing California’s complex mining operations.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Although those who migrated to California in 1849 dreamed of finding gold nuggets in streambeds, the state’s richest deposits were buried","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"deeply in rock, beyond the reach of individual prospectors. ______ by 1852, many had given up their fortune-hunting dreams and gone to work for","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"one of the large companies capable of managing California’s complex mining operations.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Furthermore,","Still,","Consequently,","Next,"]},{"id":"973632d2","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In North America, woodlands have expanded into areas that were once grasslands. Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell of Wichita State University investigated whether woodland expansion is related to changes in climate. Rogers and Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past and indexed the age of the trees with historical climate data to see if tree populations and climate were correlated. Tree population growth was associated with dry intervals. Droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion. The student wants to emphasize the aim of the research study.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In North America, woodlands have expanded into areas that were once grasslands.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell of Wichita State University investigated whether woodland expansion is related to changes in","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"climate.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Rogers and Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past and indexed the age of the trees with","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"historical climate data to see if tree populations and climate were correlated.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, researchers at Wichita State University, wanted to know if woodland expansion is related to changes in climate.","Thanks to the work done by Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell, we now know that droughts may have played a role in woodland expansion.","Wichita State University researchers have determined that tree population growth was associated with dry intervals.","Thomas Rogers and F. Leland Russell analyzed core samples from oak trees on a site that was not wooded in the past, indexing the age of the trees with historical climate data."]},{"id":"2b5e0731","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"With darkness falling, a mother elephant loses sight of her calf and wants to make sure it is safe. ______ she releases an infrasonic call for the calf to hear. Infrasonic sound is below the range of human hearing, but many animals can hear these sounds from several miles away.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"With darkness falling, a mother elephant loses sight of her calf and wants to make sure it is safe. ______ she releases an infrasonic call for the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"calf to hear. Infrasonic sound is below the range of human hearing, but many animals can hear these sounds from several miles away.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For example,","For this reason,","Nowadays,","Similarly,"]}];
